From: AmberELEC battery coulomb fuel gauge
Subject: [PATCH] rk817_battery: measured OCV table, coulomb SoC, idle re-anchor, fcc learning

Supersedes the earlier raw-voltage capacity workaround. The RG351MP shipped with
a generic placeholder ocv_table (shared verbatim across rg351mp/rg351v/xu10),
bat_res=100 and a wrong design_capacity, and the vendor relax/OCV recalibration
never triggers under emulation load, so the coulomb gauge drifted and AmberELEC
reported a raw battery voltage that ratchets to 100% while charging.

Device tree (default + timing_fix):
- ocv_table measured on an RG351MP from a full IR-compensated discharge
  (tools/ocv_analyze.py); real Li-Ion shape incl. the flat bottom.
- bat_res 100 -> 154 mOhm (measured). design_capacity/qmax kept at the nameplate
  (3500/3700); the actual (aged) capacity is learned at runtime.

Driver:
- POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CAPACITY / STATUS / uevent report the coulomb SoC (rsoc).
- rk817_bat_idle_ocv_calib(): re-anchor the coulomb counter to the IR-compensated
  OCV (V + |I|*bat_res) when discharging at low steady current; only pulls DOWN
  (SoC cannot rise while discharging), avoiding spurious jumps at table top.
- rk817_bat_fcc_learn(): learn real full-charge capacity by counting coulombs
  from CHARGE_FINISH to empty and persist to NEW_FCC, tracking ageing. Own
  integrator, clamp [design/2, qmax], a recharge voids the measurement.
- The obsolete voltage-derived capacity estimator is dropped; learned fcc +
  progress are exposed read-only at sysfs fcc.
